# Reasoning Models

A bit before GPT-4 dropped, researchers figured out that if you tell a model to "explain your reasoning," it gets _waaay_ better at hard problems. This showed up in early [chain-of-thought](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain-of-thought_prompting) prompting experiments and turned out to be weirdly effective for math, logic, and complex coding problems.

> [!dax]
>
> It's funny that this "breakthrough" was basically: "Hey, what if we let the model, you know, ~~burn more tokens~~ think longer out loud?" No new architecture, no fancy math – just let it ramble a bit before answering. While it's slower (and more expensive), it can also genuinely solve problems that it would fail on before.

## It's Built Into APIs

At its core, chain-of-thought started as a prompting trick. The big providers have since turned that idea into a first-class API feature. You can set a "reasoning effort" level that tells the model how much thinking budget to spend before answering.

OpenCode exposes that tuning through [variants](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/models#variants). A variant is a named configuration for a given model that changes settings like reasoning effort, thinking budget, and "verbosity" (how detailed the responses are).

Variant names are **model-specific**, so `low`, `high`, or `max` may or may not exist for each model. OpenCode makes it easy by showing only the variants available for the model you've selected.

You can also define or override variants explicitly in your OpenCode config:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "providers": {
    "openai": {
      "models": {
        "gpt-5": {
          "variants": [
            {
              "id": "poindexter",
              "settings": {
                "reasoningEffort": "high",
                "textVerbosity": "high",
              },
            },
            {
              "id": "grug",
              "settings": {
                "reasoningEffort": "low",
              },
            },
          ],
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

**Stick with the default variant most of the time.** If you need to, you can use [[Ctrl]]+[[T]] to cycle through the available variants, or `/thinking` to select one from a list.

![opencode thinking selected](/media/storage.googleapis.com/qvault-webapp-dynamic-assets/course_assets/XFlKXCX-850x220.png)

## Fast Mode

OpenAI also offers a "[fast mode](https://openai.com/api-fast-mode/)," and [Claude now has it too](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/fast-mode), which tells the provider to process tokens more quickly, but at a higher price per token.

> [!lane]
>
> If you're on some kind of subscription plan, and you're not using up your quota... use fast mode. Simple as.

## Assignment

**Use `high` reasoning** (if your model supports it) to fix a bug with how Sloperator interprets negative division. This SlopLang code:

```slop
hallucinate(-7 / 2)✨
```

prints `-4`, but it _should_ truncate toward zero and print `-3` instead.

1. [ ] Confirm that your selected thinking variant is `high`. If needed, use [[Ctrl]]+[[T]] to set it.
2. [ ] Tell OpenCode to fix the division bug.
3. [ ] Review the diff of the changes.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.

_After submitting, use [[Ctrl]]+[[T]] again (if needed) to return to your usual thinking variant. For DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Free, stick with `high`._

# Create a Custom Tool

Like other OpenCode config, tools (and the plugins that define them) can be _project-specific_ or apply to _all_ your _personal_ projects:

- **Project-specific tools** go in `.opencode/plugins/` in the repo. Assuming you allow that folder to be tracked, the tools will also be _shared among collaborators_.
- **Personal tools** go in `~/.config/opencode/plugins/` for use across many projects.

## Plugins Are TypeScript

OpenCode loads TypeScript and JavaScript plugins directly, but we recommend using TypeScript so that [`Plugin.define()`](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins) has nice type information.

Now, local plugin dependencies are **not** installed automatically. `Plugin.define()` needs to have `@opencode/plugin` listed in a `package.json`, for example:

```sh
cd .opencode
bun add @opencode/plugin@2.0.3
```

With that dependency available, you create a plugin file and register the tool with `ctx.tool.transform()`. For example:

```ts
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "applymaxxing.database-tool",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    await ctx.tool.transform((tools) => {
      tools.add({
        name: "database",
        description: "Query the project database",
        input: {
          type: "object",
          properties: {
            query: { type: "string" },
          },
          required: ["query"],
          additionalProperties: false,
        },
        options: { codemode: false },
        execute: async (input) => {
          const { query } = input as { query: string };
          return { content: `Executed query: ${query}` };
        },
      });
    });
  },
});
```

If that file lives at `.opencode/plugins/database.ts`, OpenCode discovers the plugin automatically and registers the `database` tool. The `id` names the _plugin_, and the `name` of the _tool_ is what OpenCode exposes to the model.

## Tool Definitions

Each tool needs a `name`, `description`, JSON-compatible `input` schema, and `execute` function. It can also declare an `output` schema. If you omit that schema, you'll need to return model-visible `content` (plain text) as the example does.

The optional second argument to `execute` contains session metadata like `sessionID`, `agent`, and `messageID`, plus a `progress` function for reporting status.

By default, custom tools use [Code Mode](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/tools/#execute), meaning the model writes JavaScript that calls tools, and OpenCode runs it through the `execute` tool. This lets the model _combine_ tool calls and process their results in code.

That said, this example sets `options: { codemode: false }` so the model will call `database` directly, just to show that you can.

> [!warning]
>
> Tool names are normalized, so duplicate names _can_ collide. Give your tool a specific name instead of trying to replace a built-in... unless you _really_ want to override one.

## Assignment

Give OpenCode a **tool for running SlopLang programs**.

1. [ ] [Install Bun](https://bun.com/docs/installation), the JavaScript/TypeScript runtime and package manager, if you don't already have it (or tell your agent to).
   ```sh
   curl -fsSL https://bun.com/install | bash
   ```
2. [ ] Exit OpenCode and reopen it from the project root in a new terminal. Confirm `bun --version` works.
3. [ ] Tell OpenCode to install `@opencode/plugin@2.0.3` in `.opencode/` with `bun`. Also have it add `node_modules/` to the project's `.gitignore` so installed dependencies stay out of version control.
4. [ ] Tell OpenCode to create a plugin, `.opencode/plugins/run-slop.ts`, using the example above for structure:
   - [ ] The plugin should define a `run_slop` tool, which accepts a file path and runs it with Sloperator, returning `stdout`, `stderr`, and `exitCode` as JSON `output`.
   - [ ] Ensure `codemode` is set to `false` so the agent calls the tool directly.
   - [ ] Have the tool run Sloperator with `uv run --no-active` from the project root, to avoid issues with the virtual environment path.
5. [ ] Tell OpenCode to use the new `run_slop` tool on `examples/hello.slop`. If the tool isn't available, you may need to use the `/reload` command. Confirm that it calls the tool and reports `hello, slop`.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.

# Configure Compaction

You can configure [compaction settings](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/compaction#settings) in `opencode.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "compaction": {
    "auto": true,
    "keep": {
      "tokens": 10000,
    },
    "buffer": 20000,
  },
}
```

- `auto`: whether to compact automatically near context limits. It defaults to `true`, and usually you want it to stay that way.
- `keep.tokens`: the _approximate_ number of tokens of recent context that OpenCode tries to preserve (in addition to the summary).
- `buffer`: the safety margin, in tokens, below the model's limit; increasing it can trigger compaction earlier.

## Assignment

Tell OpenCode to **enable automatic compaction** with `keep.tokens` set to `10000` and `buffer` set to `20000` in `opencode.jsonc`.

Then tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.

# Configure MCP

You [configure MCP servers](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/mcp-servers) in your OpenCode config:

- Project-level: `opencode.jsonc` (in the project root)
- Global: `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc`

It ends up looking something like this:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "servers": {
      "context7": {
        "type": "remote",
        "url": "https://mcp.context7.com/mcp",
        "oauth": false,
        "headers": {
          "CONTEXT7_API_KEY": "{env:CONTEXT7_API_KEY}",
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

[Context7](https://context7.com/docs/overview) is a remote MCP documentation server that gives current library/framework docs and examples on demand.

Keep credentials out of the file with `{env:CONTEXT7_API_KEY}`. OpenCode reads the value from the environment when it loads the config.

## Authentication

Most remote MCP servers require authentication of some sort. After all, the big idea behind MCP is usually to provide "programmatic" access to some data or service, and those services are almost always gated behind some kind of auth.

[API keys](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/API_key) are common for internal/dev tools for their simplicity. They require you to manage the key yourself and don't have a built-in flow for refreshing tokens.

Context7 also offers access without an API key, but at a [lower rate limit](https://context7.com/docs/api-guide#rate-limits).

## Assignment

**Connect OpenCode to Context7** so it can look up library documentation.

1. [ ] Tell OpenCode to add a project-level remote server named `context7` at `https://mcp.context7.com/mcp`. Use its public, rate-limited connection without an API-key header.
2. [ ] Use Context7 to look up how pytest runs the same test with different inputs using `parametrize`. Inspect the tool result and confirm the linked source describes that behavior.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root. Connection failures carry no penalty.

# OAuth

OpenCode supports two forms of authentication:

- API-key-style auth through custom headers
- [OAuth flows](https://oauth.net/2/) that OpenCode can kick off and manage

For simple OAuth-enabled servers, OpenCode can detect the auth requirement, kick off the flow, and store the resulting tokens for future use. The config is usually simple:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "servers": {
      "notion": {
        "type": "remote",
        "url": "https://mcp.notion.com/mcp",
      },
    },
  },
}
```

When the server reports that it needs authentication, start the sign-in flow from OpenCode's MCP interface or with `opencode mcp auth <name>`. Open the authorization URL it gives you in a browser.

> [!info]
>
> OpenCode stores the resulting credentials outside your project configuration. You shouldn't need to manage that storage directly. Use `opencode mcp logout <name>` when you want to remove credentials.

## More Complex Flows

Where this gets a _bit_ hairier is if the MCP server doesn't support [Dynamic Client Registration](https://oauth.net/2/dynamic-client-registration/). In that case (like with the GitHub MCP), you need to register and provide a client ID and secret in the config (guh):

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "servers": {
      "github": {
        "type": "remote",
        "url": "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/",
        "oauth": {
          "client_id": "{env:GITHUB_MCP_CLIENT_ID}",
          "client_secret": "{env:GITHUB_MCP_CLIENT_SECRET}",
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## CLI Commands

If you're having trouble with [authentication](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/mcp-servers#oauth), here are a few of the commands that can help you debug:

- `opencode mcp list`: List all servers and their authorization status.
- `opencode mcp auth <name>`: Authenticate with an MCP server.
- `opencode mcp logout <name>`: Remove stored credentials.

## Assignment

**Give OpenCode access to a Notion page** through OAuth, then have it read the page.

1. [ ] Sign in to [Notion](https://www.notion.com/) or create a free account. Create a page titled `Sloperator Notes` with the sentence `The slop must flow.`
2. [ ] Tell OpenCode to add the `notion` server shown above to the **project** config.
3. [ ] Run `/mcps`, select Notion, and complete the browser sign-in flow. Grant access to the workspace containing your test page.
4. [ ] Tell OpenCode to find and read `Sloperator Notes` through Notion's tools. Confirm it returns your sentence.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root. Connection failures carry no penalty.

