# Plugins

[Plugins](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins) extend OpenCode _itself_. They run code inside the agent harness, kinda like editor extensions for VS Code or Neovim.

Plugins can do things like:

- Hook into tool execution
- Modify model context before a model call
- Add support for a new authentication flow
- Register new agents, commands, skills, or tools

For example, maybe you want to:

- Save all TODOs generated by the agent to a GitHub issue tracker
- Keep a local audit log of tool calls
- Force a custom "initialize" script to run before each new session is started

> [!dax]
>
> For personal use, I just make my OpenCode's default experience match what I want, so I don't need plugins. That said, if you're trying to share a specific experience across a team or publicly, they can make a lot of sense.

## How Plugins Work

Each OpenCode plugin has a unique `id` and (typically) a `setup` function. Export the result of `Plugin.define` as the module's default export:

```ts
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "bootdev.example",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    // Register transforms, hooks, or tools here
  },
});
```

For example, this plugin keeps a local audit log so you can inspect which tools the agent used and whether they succeeded:

```ts
import { appendFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "bootdev.audit-log",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    const logPath = join(ctx.location.directory, "audit.jsonl");

    await ctx.tool.hook("execute.after", async (event) => {
      const entry = {
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
        sessionID: event.sessionID,
        tool: event.tool,
        status: event.status,
      };

      try {
        await appendFile(logPath, JSON.stringify(entry) + "\n");
      } catch (error) {
        console.error("could not write audit log:", error);
      }
    });
  },
});
```

> [!info]
>
> `execute.after` is a [hook](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins#hooks) that runs the provided code after any tool call succeeds or fails. We'll get into hooks a bit later.

# Loading Plugins

OpenCode automatically loads `.ts` and `.js` plugin files from these directories:

- Project-level: `.opencode/plugins/`
- Global: `~/.config/opencode/plugins/`

Project plugins are for _team_ behavior in a given repo; global plugins (i.e., global to your system user account) are personal.

For example, the plugin that maintains an audit log for a project belongs at `.opencode/plugins/audit-log.ts`:

```ts
import { appendFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "bootdev.audit-log",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    const logPath = join(ctx.location.directory, "audit.jsonl");

    await ctx.tool.hook("execute.after", async (event) => {
      const entry = {
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
        sessionID: event.sessionID,
        tool: event.tool,
        status: event.status,
      };

      try {
        await appendFile(logPath, JSON.stringify(entry) + "\n");
      } catch (error) {
        console.error("could not write audit log:", error);
      }
    });
  },
});
```

## Plugin Dependencies

Since the plugin code imports from [`@opencode/plugin`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@opencode/plugin), you'll also need to add a `package.json` file in the `.opencode` directory with that dependency listed:

```json
{
  "name": "course-learn-ai-coding",
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "dependencies": {
    "@opencode/plugin": "2.0.3"
  }
}
```

Run `bun install` from `.opencode` to install local plugin dependencies. _You'll want to `.gitignore` the `node_modules` directory_.

## Assignment

Use a local plugin to **log commands that interact with the playground server**.

1. [ ] Tell OpenCode to create `.opencode/plugins/playground-log.ts` using `@opencode/plugin@2.0.3`. Before the `shell` tool runs a command containing `playground/server.py`, the plugin should append `playground shell request` to `logs/plugin-playground.log`.
2. [ ] `/reload` OpenCode, then tell it to start the playground on port `8081` using its `shell` tool. Have it show you the log entry and confirm the server responds.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.

# Plugin Transforms

[Transforms](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins#transforms) let plugins change the definitions OpenCode uses for tools, agents, models, and other features. For example, a plugin can adjust a tool's description or choose a default agent.

This is useful when you're packaging a customized OpenCode experience for a team. The plugin can inspect existing definitions and apply changes in code.

## Updating a Tool

Suppose you're working on a project that includes generated files. This plugin adds a reminder to the `read` tool's description:

```ts
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "bootdev.generated-files",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    await ctx.tool.transform((tools) => {
      tools.update("read", (tool) => {
        tool.description +=
          "\nFor generated files, find the source template before proposing edits.";
      });
    });
  },
});
```

`ctx.tool.transform()` lets the plugin inspect and modify OpenCode's tool definitions. `tools.update("read", ...)` then finds the `read` tool and makes the specified changes.

## Assignment

1. [ ] Tell OpenCode to add a tool transform to `.opencode/plugins/run-slop.ts`, after the existing registration. Have it append this instruction to `run_slop`'s description:

   > Use this tool to run individual `.slop` examples when checking language behavior. Use the test suite for regression testing.

2. [ ] `/reload` OpenCode and start a new session. Ask it to report `run_slop`'s description and confirm the instruction appears. Review the diff: the tool's implementation should be unchanged.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.

# Plugin Tools

OpenCode registers every custom tool through a plugin. A published plugin can bundle tools with commands, skills, agents, or hooks so other developers can install the whole extension at once.

Use a skill or agent when a workflow needs only _instructions_. Add a tool when it _needs code_, for example to query an API or database.

To register a tool in a plugin, call `tools.add()` inside a [`ctx.tool.transform()`](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins#tools) callback. Here's a plugin that fetches the top comment on a [Hacker News](https://github.com/HackerNews/API) post:

```ts
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";

type HNItem = {
  text?: string;
  kids?: number[];
};

async function getItem(id: number): Promise<HNItem | null> {
  const res = await fetch(
    `https://hacker-news.firebaseio.com/v0/item/${id}.json`,
  );
  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error(`failed to fetch Hacker News item ${id}`);
  }
  return (await res.json()) as HNItem | null;
}

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "bootdev.hn-top-comment",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    await ctx.tool.transform((tools) => {
      tools.add({
        name: "hn_top_comment",
        description: "Get the top comment for a Hacker News post by ID",
        input: {
          type: "object",
          properties: {
            post_id: { type: "number" },
          },
          required: ["post_id"],
          additionalProperties: false,
        },
        output: {
          type: "object",
          properties: {
            comment: { type: "string" },
          },
          required: ["comment"],
          additionalProperties: false,
        },
        execute: async (input) => {
          const { post_id } = input as { post_id: number };
          const post = await getItem(post_id);
          if (!post) {
            throw new Error(`Hacker News post ${post_id} was not found`);
          }
          const topCommentID = post.kids?.[0];
          const item = topCommentID ? await getItem(topCommentID) : null;
          const comment =
            item?.text ?? "The top comment's text is unavailable.";
          return {
            output: { comment },
            content: comment,
          };
        },
        options: { codemode: false },
      });
    });
  },
});
```

> [!warning]
>
> Tool names are normalized, and duplicate names can collide. Use a specific name or namespace instead of trying to replace a built-in tool.

## Assignment

**Create a tool that reports the results of running every example.**

1. [ ] Tell OpenCode to create `.opencode/plugins/example-outputs.ts` with an `example_outputs` tool. It should run every `examples/*.slop` file and return an `examples` array. Each entry should contain the filename as `name`, the process's exit code as `exitCode`, and its first output line as `firstLine` (without the newline).
2. [ ] `/reload` OpenCode and tell it to call `example_outputs`. Inspect the tool result: every example should exit successfully. For example, `factorial.slop` should report `5! =` as its first line, and `hello.slop` should report `hello, slop`.

Tell OpenCode to **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.
